    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Foreign National Loans: How Non-US Residents Can Buy Property in America</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8 font-bold">For the International Buyer Who Wants to Own US Property and Isn't Sure Where to Start</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If you live outside the United States and want to purchase property here, whether as an investment, a vacation home, or a future base, a foreign national loan is the product designed for your situation. US property ownership is open to non-residents, and there is a specific lending category built around buyers who have no US credit history, no US income documentation, and no Social Security number.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What Makes a Foreign National Loan Different From Any Other Mortgage</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A foreign national loan is a Non-QM mortgage product for buyers who reside outside the United States and do not have the documentation profile that US-based mortgage programs require. No Social Security number, no US tax returns, no US credit history: none of the standard inputs that conventional underwriting depends on are available for this borrower. The lender builds their qualification picture from a different set of documents, primarily the borrower's foreign income and assets, their passport and visa documentation, and a credit report from their home country if one is available.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why US Lenders Don't All Understand This Product</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's what trips up most foreign national buyers early in the process. They contact a general mortgage lender, get told the product doesn't exist or that they can't help, and assume US property ownership isn't possible for someone in their situation. Foreign national loans are a specialized Non-QM product that not every lender offers. The lenders who do offer them have built specific underwriting guidelines, documentation checklists, and processing workflows around international buyers. Working with a lender who doesn't have that infrastructure leads to frustration and delays that have nothing to do with your actual qualification.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The product exists. Working with a lender who has that infrastructure is what makes the difference.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Foreign National Loans Are Structured</h2>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Down payment.</strong> Foreign national loans typically require 25 to 40% down, reflecting the higher risk profile of a borrower with no US financial footprint. The down payment must be documented and sourced, meaning you'll need to show where the funds came from.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Income documentation.</strong> Lenders generally accept foreign income documentation including pay stubs, employer letters, or tax returns from your home country, translated into English where required. Self-employed foreign nationals typically need additional documentation of business ownership and income.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Credit.</strong> If your home country has a credit reporting system, a credit report from that system is typically required. For buyers from countries without formal credit bureaus, lenders may accept alternative documentation of financial responsibility.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Property type.</strong> Most foreign national programs are available for single-family homes, condos, and multi-family properties up to four units. Some lenders extend to investment property purchases. Primary residence purchases are less common under foreign national programs because the borrower by definition resides outside the US.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Reserves.</strong> Lenders typically require significant liquid reserves, often 12 months or more of total monthly payments, held in a verifiable account. These reserves demonstrate financial stability in the absence of US income documentation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Work with a lender who specializes in foreign national loans before you do anything else.</strong> The documentation requirements, down payment thresholds, and underwriting process differ enough from standard mortgages that general lender guidance on this product is often incomplete or inaccurate.</li>
      <li><strong>Gather your income documentation from your home country.</strong> Two years of tax returns or equivalent income verification, translated into English, is the standard starting point for most programs.</li>
      <li><strong>Obtain a credit report from your home country if one is available.</strong> Ask your lender what format they require and whether a certified translation is needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Document your down payment funds clearly.</strong> Foreign national lenders scrutinize the source of down payment funds carefully. Bank statements showing the funds have been held in your account for a defined period are typically required.</li>
      <li><strong>Understand the reserve requirement before you finalize your purchase price.</strong> The down payment is not the only liquid capital you need at closing. Reserves held after closing are a separate requirement, and that total affects how much you can comfortably put toward the purchase itself.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Start the Conversation With the Right Lender</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Use Cliffco's Mortgage Readiness Checkup below to see where your application currently stands based on your income, assets, and credit profile. A Cliffco loan officer can then walk you through the documentation requirements specific to your country of residence and help you understand what purchase price and property type are realistic for your situation.</p>

    <!-- Embedded lite Mortgage Readiness Checkup -->
    <div class="mt-12 border border-[var(--color-accent)]/25 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-accent)]/8 px-6 py-5 border-b border-[var(--color-accent)]/15">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Check your mortgage readiness.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Answer five questions to see where you stand and what your next move should be.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Credit score range</label>
            <select id="art-mr-credit" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="780">760 or above</option>
              <option value="740">720 – 759</option>
              <option value="700" selected>680 – 719</option>
              <option value="660">640 – 679</option>
              <option value="630">620 – 639</option>
              <option value="580">Below 620</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Employment type</label>
            <select id="art-mr-emp" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="w2">W-2 employee</option>
              <option value="self">Self-employed</option>
              <option value="1099">1099 / freelance</option>
              <option value="other">Other / retired</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Gross monthly income ($)</label>
            <input id="art-mr-income" type="number" value="8000" min="0" step="500" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Monthly debt payments ($)</label>
            <input id="art-mr-debt" type="number" value="500" min="0" step="50" class="art-calc-input" />
            <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Car, student loans, credit cards, etc.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Liquid savings ($)</label>
            <input id="art-mr-savings" type="number" value="20000" min="0" step="1000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-mr-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Check My Readiness</button>

